(function(){
  window.AP_LEARNING=window.AP_LEARNING||{};
  const M=(id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visualTitle,visualItems,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,prompt,options)=>({id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual:{title:visualTitle,kind:'concept-map',items:visualItems.map(x=>({label:x[0],detail:x[1]}))},mnemonic:{title:mnemonic[0],cue:mnemonic[1],explanation:mnemonic[2]},goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity:{prompt,options:options.map(x=>({text:x[0],correct:x[1],feedback:x[2]}))}});
  window.AP_LEARNING['lesson-9']={version:1,title:'The Muscular System',chapter:9,duration:'75–95 minutes',
    outcomes:['Compare skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle.','Trace skeletal muscle from connective-tissue covering to sarcomere filaments.','Explain excitation–contraction coupling and the sliding-filament model.','Compare ATP sources, muscle tone and contraction types.','Apply movement and naming rules to major skeletal muscles.'],
    modules:[
      {id:'muscle-map',title:'From whole muscle to filament',summary:'Muscle organization is nested: organ, fascicle, fiber, myofibril, sarcomere, filament.',moments:[
        M('l9-m1','Three muscle tissues','Control, striation, cell shape and location separate the three types.',[
          'Skeletal muscle is striated, usually voluntary, and moves the skeleton. Cardiac muscle is striated but involuntary and forms the heart wall. Smooth muscle lacks visible striations, is involuntary, and moves material through hollow organs and blood vessels.',
          'All three convert chemical energy into force, yet their cell organization and control differ. Cardiac activity is influenced by the autonomic nervous system; this does not make cardiac tissue smooth muscle.'
        ],['Skeletal: striated and voluntary.','Cardiac: striated and involuntary.','Smooth: nonstriated and involuntary.','Cardiac and smooth activity are autonomically influenced.'],'Three-way comparison',[['Skeletal','Bones; voluntary'],['Cardiac','Heart; involuntary'],['Smooth','Hollow organs; involuntary']],['SCS','Skeletal · Cardiac · Smooth','Move from body wall to heart to hollow organs.'],'A tissue can be striated without being voluntarily controlled.','Avoid promising that a food or supplement can selectively change one muscle type.',
        'Which striated muscle is involuntary?',[['Cardiac',true,'Correct. Cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary.'],['Skeletal',false,'Skeletal muscle is usually voluntary.'],['Smooth',false,'Smooth muscle is involuntary but not striated.']]),
        M('l9-m2','The nested organization','Connective-tissue layers package force-producing cells.',[
          'Epimysium surrounds the whole muscle. Perimysium surrounds a fascicle, and endomysium surrounds each muscle fiber. A fiber contains many myofibrils, which are repeating chains of sarcomeres.',
          'Within each sarcomere, thin actin and thick myosin filaments form the banding pattern. The light I band contains thin filaments; the dark A band spans thick filaments. Tendons transmit the combined pull to bone.'
        ],['Epimysium: whole muscle.','Perimysium: fascicle.','Endomysium: fiber.','Myofibrils contain repeating sarcomeres.','Actin is thin; myosin is thick.'],'Zoom sequence',[['Muscle','Epimysium'],['Fascicle','Perimysium'],['Fiber','Endomysium'],['Myofibril','Sarcomere chain'],['Sarcomere','Actin + myosin']],['EPE: entire, packet, each','Epimysium · Perimysium · Endomysium','Move from the entire muscle to packets to each fiber.'],'A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers, not one fiber or one myofibril.','Adequate dietary protein supports whole-body protein turnover; it does not bypass training, recovery or clinical factors.',
        'What surrounds a fascicle?',[['Perimysium',true,'Correct. Perimysium wraps each fascicle.'],['Epimysium',false,'That surrounds the whole muscle.'],['Endomysium',false,'That surrounds an individual fiber.']])
      ]},
      {id:'contraction',title:'Turn a nerve signal into force',summary:'Electrical events release calcium; calcium permits actin–myosin cycling; ATP powers and resets it.',moments:[
        M('l9-m3','Neuromuscular activation','Acetylcholine starts a muscle-fiber action potential.',[
          'When a motor-neuron impulse reaches the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine is released. It binds receptors on the sarcolemma, changing ion permeability; sodium entry helps depolarize the membrane and launch an action potential.',
          'The signal travels along the sarcolemma and down T tubules. This triggers the sarcoplasmic reticulum to release calcium around the myofibrils. The electrical message has now become a chemical permission signal for contraction.'
        ],['Acetylcholine is the neuromuscular transmitter.','Sodium is concentrated outside the resting fiber.','The action potential enters T tubules.','The sarcoplasmic reticulum releases calcium.'],'Signal relay',[['Motor neuron','Impulse arrives'],['ACh','Crosses junction'],['Sarcolemma/T tubule','Conducts signal'],['SR','Releases Ca²⁺']],['A-T-C','Acetylcholine · T tubule · Calcium','Recall the activation sequence.'],'Calcium release here is intracellular signaling, not a claim that an acute calcium supplement directly causes a stronger contraction.','Explain nutrient roles as background physiology while keeping performance and symptom claims evidence based.',
        'What ion is released by the sarcoplasmic reticulum after T-tubule activation?',[['Calcium',true,'Correct. Calcium exposes actin sites through regulatory proteins.'],['Sodium',false,'Sodium helps depolarize the membrane but is not the SR signal here.'],['Chloride',false,'It is not the key coupling ion in this sequence.']]),
        M('l9-m4','Sliding filaments and ATP','Filaments slide past one another; they do not become shorter.',[
          'Calcium binds the regulatory system associated with troponin and tropomyosin, exposing binding sites on actin. Energized myosin heads attach, pivot, and pull thin filaments toward the sarcomere center.',
          'ATP binding releases myosin from actin; ATP breakdown re-cocks the head for another cycle. As cycling repeats, Z lines approach, the I band and H zone narrow, and the A band stays the same length.'
        ],['Calcium exposes binding sites.','Myosin forms cross-bridges with actin.','ATP is needed for detachment and re-energizing.','The A band remains constant.'],'Cross-bridge cycle',[['Expose','Calcium shifts regulators'],['Attach','Myosin binds actin'],['Pull','Power stroke'],['Release','ATP binds'],['Reset','ATP is hydrolyzed']],['CAPRR','Calcium · Attach · Pull · Release · Reset','Use the cycle rather than memorizing isolated steps.'],'Rigor after death reflects loss of ATP-dependent cross-bridge release, illustrating ATP’s role in relaxation.','Energy availability matters to muscle physiology, but fatigue has multiple neural, metabolic and environmental contributors.',
        'During contraction, which band remains the same length?',[['A band',true,'Correct. It reflects the length of thick filaments.'],['I band',false,'The I band narrows as overlap increases.'],['H zone',false,'The H zone narrows and may disappear.']])
      ]},
      {id:'energy-control',title:'Fuel, tension and adaptation',summary:'Muscles regenerate ATP through overlapping systems and express force in different ways.',moments:[
        M('l9-m5','Three routes to ATP','Stored ATP is small; phosphocreatine, glycolysis and aerobic metabolism replenish it.',[
          'Muscle stores only a brief supply of ATP. Phosphocreatine rapidly transfers a phosphate to ADP and is especially useful during short, intense work. Anaerobic glycolysis produces ATP quickly from glucose but yields lactate when pyruvate cannot be processed aerobically at the same rate.',
          'Aerobic metabolism in mitochondria uses oxygen and fuel molecules to generate much more ATP, supporting longer-duration activity. All systems overlap; their relative contribution shifts with intensity, duration, training and recovery.'
        ],['Phosphocreatine rapidly buffers ATP.','Glycolysis can proceed without oxygen.','Aerobic metabolism yields more ATP over time.','Lactate is associated with anaerobic processing in this text’s framework.'],'Energy continuum',[['Stored ATP','Seconds'],['Phosphocreatine','Rapid high power'],['Anaerobic glycolysis','Fast, limited yield'],['Aerobic metabolism','Slower, sustained yield']],['PGA','Phosphocreatine · Glycolysis · Aerobic','Order the major replenishment routes from rapid to sustained.'],'Phosphocreatine is the phosphorylated molecule named in the source quiz, whereas creatine alone is not the immediate phosphate donor.','Meal timing and adequacy can support training, but individualized sport recommendations depend on context and scope.',
        'What molecule rapidly donates phosphate to regenerate ATP in muscle?',[['Phosphocreatine',true,'Correct. It serves as a rapid high-energy phosphate reserve.'],['Actin',false,'Actin is a contractile protein.'],['Lactate',false,'Lactate is not the immediate phosphate donor.']]),
        M('l9-m6','Tone and contraction types','Length change and tension are separate features.',[
          'Muscle tone is a low level of ongoing activation that helps posture and readiness. In an isotonic contraction, tension moves a load and muscle length changes: concentric action shortens; eccentric action lengthens under tension.',
          'In an isometric contraction, tension rises but overall muscle length and the joint angle remain relatively constant. Repeated overload can promote hypertrophy; disuse can lead to atrophy.'
        ],['Tone supports posture.','Concentric shortens under load.','Eccentric lengthens under load.','Isometric creates tension without visible shortening.','Disuse promotes atrophy.'],'Tension map',[['Concentric','Shorten'],['Eccentric','Lengthen under load'],['Isometric','Hold length'],['Tone','Baseline activation']],['Metric means length','IsoMETRIC keeps the measurement similar','Isotonic involves movement in the simplified textbook contrast.'],'Holding a weight still is isometric; lifting it is typically concentric, not isometric.','When appetite or access limits intake during rehabilitation, coordinate nutrition support with the care team rather than prescribing exercise.',
        'A person holds a grocery bag motionless with the elbow fixed. Which contraction dominates?',[['Isometric',true,'Correct. Tension is present without an obvious length change.'],['Concentric',false,'That would shorten the active muscle to move the load.'],['Atrophy',false,'Atrophy is loss of muscle bulk, not a contraction type.']])
      ]},
      {id:'muscle-actions',title:'Read muscle names and actions',summary:'Names often reveal location, shape, fiber direction, attachment or action.',moments:[
        M('l9-m7','Origin, insertion and teamwork','Movement emerges from groups, not one muscle acting alone.',[
          'The origin is usually the less movable attachment; the insertion is usually the more movable attachment where the effect is seen. During an action, the prime mover or agonist supplies the main force, an antagonist opposes or controls it, and synergists assist or stabilize.',
          'Muscle names can encode clues: rectus means straight, oblique signals diagonal fibers, maximus indicates large size, and terms such as flexor, adductor or levator describe action.'
        ],['Insertion is typically the movable attachment.','Agonist is the prime mover.','Antagonist opposes or controls.','Synergists assist.','Names often predict action or anatomy.'],'Team roles',[['Agonist','Main action'],['Antagonist','Opposes/controls'],['Synergist','Assists'],['Origin','Less movable'],['Insertion','More movable']],['AIS','Agonist acts · antagonist inhibits/controls · synergist supports','Use roles rather than memorizing pairs.'],'The same muscle may be an agonist in one movement and an antagonist in another.','Functional language can identify tasks a client finds difficult without assuming weakness, injury or motivation.',
        'What is the movable attachment called?',[['Insertion',true,'Correct. The effect of contraction is usually seen at the insertion.'],['Origin',false,'The origin is usually less movable.'],['Fascicle',false,'A fascicle is a bundle of fibers.']]),
        M('l9-m8','Anchor muscles by function','Connect a familiar action to each name.',[
          'Frontalis raises the eyebrows; rectus abdominis flexes the trunk; the superior rectus elevates the eye. Flexor digitorum longus flexes the toes, while tibialis anterior contributes to dorsiflexion.',
          'Action words generalize: abductors move away from the midline, adductors return toward it, levators raise, depressors lower, flexors decrease an angle and extensors increase it. Learn the rule first, then the regional example.'
        ],['Frontalis raises eyebrows.','Rectus abdominis flexes trunk.','Flexor digitorum longus flexes toes.','Superior rectus raises the eye.','Tibialis anterior dorsiflexes.'],'Name-to-action anchors',[['Frontalis','Forehead/eyebrows'],['Rectus abdominis','Trunk flexion'],['Flexor digitorum longus','Toe flexion'],['Superior rectus','Eye elevation'],['Tibialis anterior','Dorsiflexion']],['Name is a clue','Region + direction + action','Decode the components before rote recall.'],'Dorsiflexion appears in both the joint and muscle lessons because movement vocabulary bridges systems.','Use task substitutions and accessible food preparation when movement is limited, in coordination with clinical recommendations.',
        'Which muscle is emphasized in a traditional sit-up?',[['Rectus abdominis',true,'Correct. It is a major trunk flexor.'],['Frontalis',false,'That raises the eyebrows.'],['Superior rectus',false,'That moves the eye.']])
      ]}
    ],
    caseStudy:{title:'Fuel is only one part of force',scenario:'A recreational athlete says fatigue during repeated lifts must mean one specific nutrient is missing.',prompts:['Trace the signal from motor neuron to calcium release.','Name the ATP systems that overlap during repeated work.','Distinguish concentric, eccentric and isometric phases.','Explain why fatigue cannot be diagnosed from the workout description alone.'],takeaway:'Connect neural activation, cross-bridge cycling, energy systems and task demands before considering nutrition within scope.'},
    recap:['Skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle differ in striation, control and location.','Epimysium, perimysium and endomysium organize muscle, fascicles and fibers.','Actin is thin and myosin thick; sarcomeres shorten by filament sliding.','Acetylcholine starts the muscle action potential; the sarcoplasmic reticulum releases calcium.','ATP powers cross-bridge release and reset.','Phosphocreatine, glycolysis and aerobic metabolism replenish ATP.','Agonists, antagonists and synergists coordinate movement.'],
    flashcards:[{front:'Fascicle',back:'Bundle of muscle fibers.',tag:'organization'},{front:'Epimysium',back:'Connective tissue around a whole muscle.',tag:'organization'},{front:'Perimysium',back:'Connective tissue around a fascicle.',tag:'organization'},{front:'Sarcomere',back:'Repeating contractile unit of a myofibril.',tag:'contraction'},{front:'Actin',back:'Thin contractile filament.',tag:'contraction'},{front:'Myosin',back:'Thick motor filament.',tag:'contraction'},{front:'Acetylcholine',back:'Neurotransmitter released at the neuromuscular junction.',tag:'activation'},{front:'Phosphocreatine',back:'Rapid high-energy phosphate reserve for ATP regeneration.',tag:'energy'},{front:'Isometric',back:'Tension without appreciable muscle shortening.',tag:'contraction'},{front:'Atrophy',back:'Decrease in muscle bulk, often associated with disuse.',tag:'adaptation'},{front:'Agonist',back:'Prime mover for an action.',tag:'movement'},{front:'Insertion',back:'Usually the more movable muscle attachment.',tag:'movement'}],
    practice:[{prompt:'Order whole muscle, fascicle, fiber, myofibril and sarcomere from largest to smallest.',answer:'Whole muscle → fascicle → fiber → myofibril → sarcomere.',rationale:'Each level is nested within the previous one.',tag:'organization'},{prompt:'What proteins dominate thin and thick filaments?',answer:'Actin is thin; myosin is thick.',rationale:'Their interaction produces force.',tag:'contraction'},{prompt:'Trace activation from the motor neuron to calcium.',answer:'Impulse → acetylcholine → sarcolemma/T tubules → sarcoplasmic-reticulum calcium release.',rationale:'This is excitation–contraction coupling.',tag:'activation'},{prompt:'What lets myosin detach from actin?',answer:'ATP binding.',rationale:'ATP is essential to cross-bridge release and reset.',tag:'contraction'},{prompt:'Why is phosphocreatine useful?',answer:'It rapidly transfers phosphate to ADP to regenerate ATP.',rationale:'It buffers immediate energy demand.',tag:'energy'},{prompt:'Contrast concentric and isometric actions.',answer:'Concentric action shortens a muscle; isometric action creates tension without appreciable length change.',rationale:'Length change distinguishes them.',tag:'contraction'},{prompt:'Which connective tissue surrounds one muscle fiber?',answer:'Endomysium.',rationale:'Endo is the innermost of the three muscle coverings.',tag:'organization'},{prompt:'What does an abductor do?',answer:'Moves a part away from the midline.',rationale:'The action term follows joint-movement language.',tag:'movement'},{prompt:'Name the prime mover and the opposing role.',answer:'Agonist and antagonist.',rationale:'They coordinate rather than simply switch on and off.',tag:'movement'},{prompt:'Why is cardiac muscle not skeletal muscle?',answer:'Both are striated, but cardiac muscle is involuntary, branching and located in the heart.',rationale:'Striation alone does not determine muscle type.',tag:'tissue'}]
  };
})();
